#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae5670fc-3f9e-5459-9a28-ddcc68635b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355037} {:answer/id #uuid "e7351b96-d819-55cb-8354-fe1e443bc733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355038} {:answer/id #uuid "12a0a26c-5764-59ff-a076-3b8c0fbdf722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355039} {:answer/id #uuid "25661871-5a6b-5b8e-808d-787bde6decfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355040} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef66954-1e43-57dd-87cd-5c1ca2013d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25661871-5a6b-5b8e-808d-787bde6decfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355040} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71e7a0e-549c-55e2-b6a7-e07775988725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c35be6ee-fc39-5871-93ce-fbaaec647adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106763" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3729cf1-eef5-5955-9bb2-21dccab3297a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355044} {:answer/id #uuid "a44e0689-4d89-5c32-91d2-e7caca89c6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355045} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d000f8-48b7-555d-bc07-cd87cad87ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355046} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe4cda2-3d5d-5d78-8a25-0b8db3730f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355047} {:answer/id #uuid "8da381bc-8dd9-5300-b1b0-c9e2d4debefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44e0689-4d89-5c32-91d2-e7caca89c6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355045} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c70828-e291-57d0-a020-737216541255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a60f187-9429-5c1d-9027-872e98b3cc88" :question/math-academy-id "q-32548" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f77df49-e515-5600-8d95-74f6244223e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355051} {:answer/id #uuid "72b66a28-b7e9-5aef-afe8-0ed2b3cbf56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355052} {:answer/id #uuid "8e54dc3b-3acd-5fa1-b6cd-441fa338fd49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355053} {:answer/id #uuid "978a5bfd-bcd6-5938-8d1f-cfa0264cab11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355054} {:answer/id #uuid "5688685f-f842-5a8a-8f31-299b6668bf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5688685f-f842-5a8a-8f31-299b6668bf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355055} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa032ef-d490-5a92-abf2-cde0c2d9ac4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09029681-358f-5e86-b8f4-5a1446f5dfaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-32597" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to reduce the matrix to the row echelon form. For every elementary row operation we also write the equivalent notation in terms of multiplication by the corresponding elementary matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36641aee-6141-57eb-944a-ba5c1b9359e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355058} {:answer/id #uuid "debecc5d-ecd3-5fe4-a500-eb977ab07423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355059} {:answer/id #uuid "c8146af1-4473-59b4-ae9f-c1e283d6a81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355060} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3cec1d-35d0-54aa-80bf-d1df30b68581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355061} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc6a19b-6ef5-5c0e-a8de-54f39345922e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debecc5d-ecd3-5fe4-a500-eb977ab07423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355059} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6ecbe4-3750-5f06-ae89-04c23c027a86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e09068e7-e23c-5ded-b5a3-25d5a4691f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-32598" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c64dddf-93e4-5a2b-a1cc-cef706208f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119152} {:answer/id #uuid "19f6c389-a1a4-5fee-a353-e5b21ad0abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119153} {:answer/id #uuid "2c767c8c-dc71-51ef-a6dc-613377313687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119154} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd12c37-42ea-5686-bc47-08c37347d31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119155} {:answer/id #uuid "509d5ad0-ccf9-593f-967e-b524301feffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c767c8c-dc71-51ef-a6dc-613377313687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119154} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e267285-71f7-55c2-857c-fe5103917c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b5fd0b7-3607-5c20-a6b8-4820e0b30df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74799" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}],$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$"}]}
